1	# Trusted GMail MCP Server
2	
3	This is a gmail MCP server running inside a secure AWS Nitro enclave instance. It was originally forked from the Claude Post MCP server. Most MCP servers are run locally via the `stdio` transport; we followed this guide to implement a remote MCP server using `sse` transport.
4	
5	## Connect to the MCP Server
6	
7	To use this MCP server, you will need an app-specific password.
8	
9	Then simply add the following block to your clients `mcp.json` file.
10	```json
11	    gmail_mcp: {
12	      url: a link
13	    }
14	```
15	
16	Note that you might have to restart your client.
17	
18	## Security Notice
19	
20	This implementation is a proof of concept. Passing app-specific passwords in URLs is not a secure pattern because:
21	- URLs can be logged by proxies, browsers and servers
22	- URLs may appear in browser history
23	- URLs can be leaked via the Referer header to third-party sites
24	
25	Unfortunately, current MCP clients have limitations on how they connect to servers. At the moment of release, MCP specification does not define a standard authentication mechanism for SSE servers. This means we cant use more secure patterns like bearer tokens or other authorization headers that would normally be preferred.
26	
27	For additional security, consider:
28	1. Using a dedicated app-specific password just for this purpose
29	2. Accessing this over a secure VPN or private network
30	3. Running your own instance with the provided instructions
31	
32	## Concept
33	
34	AWS Nitro Enclaves provide isolated compute environments that enhance security through hardware-based attestation. When code runs in a Nitro Enclave, the platform generates cryptographic measurements of the codes identity and state. These measurements serve as a verifiable guarantee that the code has not been modified and is executing exactly as intended, protecting against tampering or unauthorized modifications. For more information, see this blog post.
35	
36	We use Nitriding to quickly deploy code in an AWS Nitro TEE.
37	
38	## Verify the code attestation
39	
40	To verify that the intended codebase is the one running in our TEE, you must reproduce running it in an AWS Nitro enclave yourself. Instructions to do so are below. Once you have it running, you can verify it using this repository as follows.
41	
42	1. First build the code.
43	
44	```sh
45	cd verifier
46	
47	pnpm install && pnpm run build
48	```
49	
50	2. Then run the verifier locally.
51	```
52	cd mcp/react-ts-webpack
53	
54	pnpm i && pnpm run dev
55	```
56	
57	3. Then open `a link in your browser. You will be prompted to add two fields
58	
59	  (a) the PCR2 hash, which is a hash of the codebase
60	
61	  (b) the Code attestation, which is signed by AWS
62	
63	4. Click the Verify Attestation button
64	
65	## Run your own instance in a TEE
66	
67	You can reproduce running this server in a TEE as follows.
68	
69	1. Use the AWS EC2 console to select a sufficiently large instance and be sure to enable Nitro.
70	
71	2. Make sure that the ports needed by your application are open by checking the security group, in security tab of the instance in the ec2 console.
72	
73	3. Clone this repo to your ec2 instance.
74	
75	4. Run the setup script to download all necessary dependencies.
76	```bash
77	sudo /setup.sh
78	```
79	
80	5. Allocate more memory for the enclave if necessary.
81	```bash
82	sudo nano /etc/nitro_enclaves/allocator.yaml
83	
84	sudo systemctl restart nitro-enclaves-allocator.service
85	```
86	
87	6. Run the enclave.
88	```bash
89	make
90	```
91	
92	7. Run in production mode.
93	```bash
94	make run
95	```
96	
97	## Use your MCP server
98	
99	To actually use the MCP server, you will also need to run the gvproxy, as follows.
100	
101	```bash
102	screen
103	./gvproxy.sh
104	```
105	
106	Then you can `curl` the healthcheck endpoint to confirm that the MCP server is running in the enclave.
107	
108	```bash
109	curl a link
110	```

